# Create a SurfDoc

A SurfDoc is markdown with two additions: required YAML frontmatter, and typed
blocks for the things markdown has no syntax for. Everything else is ordinary
markdown, on purpose — a SurfDoc that is never rendered is still a good
document.

## 1. Frontmatter (required)

Open the file with a `---` fence on line 1. Required keys:

| Key | Value |
|---|---|
| `title` | quoted string; the document's name |
| `type` | what kind of document: `plan`, `spec`, `context`, `note`, `report` |
| `status` | `draft`, `active`, `superseded`, `archived` |
| `updated` | `YYYY-MM-DD`, the date the content was last verified |

Strongly recommended:

| Key | Value |
|---|---|
| `answers` | a list of the questions this document settles — the relevance hooks a session digest reads |
| `tags` | a list of short lowercase labels |

`answers:` is what makes a document findable by an agent that has not read it.
Write the questions a future session would actually type, not a summary.

## 2. Headings

Standard markdown. One `#` title matching the frontmatter `title`, `##` for
sections, `###` for subsections. Do not invent heading syntax; do not skip
levels.

## 3. Tables

Standard markdown pipe tables. Keep them narrow enough to read unrendered — a
table wider than about a hundred characters is a list in disguise.

## 4. Typed blocks

Blocks open with `::name[attributes]` and close with `::`. Use one only when
markdown genuinely cannot express the content: a diagram, a chart, a figure, a
callout that must survive a re-render. Attributes are `key="value"` pairs.

Every block must **degrade gracefully**: a reader with no renderer sees the
block's text content and still understands the document. Never put load-bearing
information only inside a block's attributes.

## 5. Diagrams and charts

Reach for a diagram when the relationship between parts is the point — a
sequence, a state machine, a dependency graph, a layout. Reach for a chart when
a number's shape is the point — a trend, a distribution, a comparison. If a
sentence says it better, write the sentence.

Give every diagram a caption that states its claim, so the claim survives even
when the drawing does not render.

## 6. What not to do

- No HTML in the body when markdown can express it.
- No inline styling; the renderer owns presentation.
- No unreferenced blocks left behind from an earlier draft.
- No absolute machine paths, host names, credentials or internal identifiers in
  a document that may be published.

## 7. Procedure

1. Decide the `type` and where the document belongs. When `home` is `surfspace`,
   create it in the workspace; otherwise write the file into the canonical tree
   named by `surfcontext.json`.
2. Write the frontmatter, including `answers:`, before the body.
3. Write the body in plain markdown; add a typed block only where it earns its
   place.
4. Verify every fact against a live source as you write it, and mark provenance
   — `measured this session` versus `recorded previously, unverified`.
5. Re-read the finished document as plain text. If it does not make sense
   unrendered, it is not finished.
